<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tigris</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tigris is one of the two great rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, flowing from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> south-east through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Its waters supported irrigation agriculture and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It runs through the history of empires from the Akkadian and Assyrian to the Seleucid, Sasanian and Abbasid, whose capital Baghdad was founded on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tigris.jpg" alt="Tigris" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Duha masood · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosul_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…755, eliminating the most dangerous rival power centre, and in 762 founded the Round City of Baghdad, Madinat al-Salam or &quot;City of Peace&quot;, on the western bank of the <b>Tigris</b> not far from the ruins of the Sasanian capital Ctesiphon, deliberately connecting Abbasid authority to Persian imperial prestige.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Akkadian Empire governed one of the most economically productive regions of the ancient world, the alluvial plain of southern Mesopotamia fed by the <b>Tigris</b> and Euphrates, and connected it through trade networks reaching from the Indus Valley Civilisation to the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aq Qoyunlu, or ‘White Sheep’ Turkmens, were a confederation of Oghuz Turkmen tribes led by the Bayandur clan, whose flocks pastured around Diyarbakır and the upper <b>Tigris</b> in the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Assyrian Empire's origins trace back to the city of Assur on the west bank of the <b>Tigris</b> in northern Mesopotamia, which emerged as an independent city-state around 2025 BCE following the collapse of the Third Dynasty of Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…remarkable careers of personal state-building, that of Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub, known in the West as Saladin, a Kurdish commander of exceptional political intelligence who transformed himself from a constrained vizier in Cairo into the master of the largest Muslim realm between the Nile and the <b>Tigris</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Old Babylonian economy rested on the agricultural productivity of the Mesopotamian alluvial plain — one of the most fertile zones of the ancient world, watered by the <b>Tigris</b> and Euphrates through an elaborate network of irrigation canals that the Babylonian state maintained and extended.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
